window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["24-482"]={"d":"24-482","id":"2025/24-482","n":"Ellingburg v. United States","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner Holsey Ellingburg, Jr.","date":"2025-06-23","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/24/24-482/363718/20250623134532060_Ellingburg%20Brief%20for%20Petitioner.pdf","side":"pet","n":66},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent United States supporting vacatur","date":"2025-06-23","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/24/24-482/363761/20250623190708188_24-482_Ellingburg_Opening_Brief_final.pdf","side":"res","n":110},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner Holsey Ellingburg, Jr.","date":"2025-09-22","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/24/24-482/375483/20250922141813336_2025.09.22%20Ellingburg%20reply%20.pdf","side":"pet","n":30}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/607us1r09_4fb4.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Apprendi v. New Jersey","cite":"530 U.S. 466","y":2000,"id":"1999/99-478","sc":1,"ar":7,"sum":"The Court held that the Due Process Clause requires that any fact that increases the penalty for a crime beyond the prescribed statutory maximum, other than the fact of a prior conviction, must be submitted to a jury and proved beyond a reasonable doubt.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"44","1":"26","2":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bearden v. Georgia","cite":"461 U.S. 660","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-6633","sc":1,"sum":"A sentencing court cannot properly revoke a defendant's probation for failure to pay a fine and make restitution, absent evidence and findings that he was somehow responsible for the failure or that alternative forms of punishment were inadequate to meet the State's interest in punishment and deterrence, and hence here the trial court erred in automatically revoking petitioner's probation and turning the fine into a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cal. Dep’t of Corr. v. Morales","cite":"514 U.S. 499","y":1995,"id":"1994/93-1462","sc":1,"sum":"The amendment's application to prisoners who committed their crimes before it was enacted does not violate the Ex Post Facto Clause.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"8","1":"4, 5, 12, 15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Caldwell v. State","cite":"55 Ala. 133","y":1876,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ala./55/133/","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Collins v. Youngblood","cite":"497 U.S. 37","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-742","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"Respondent was convicted in a Texas state court of aggravated sexual abuse and sentenced to life imprisonment and a $10,000 fine. After his conviction and sentence were affirmed on direct appeal, he applied for a writ of habeas corpus in state court, arguing that Texas law did not authorize both a fine and prison term for his offense, and thus that his judgment and sentence were void and he was entitled to a new …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13, 26","1":"15, 29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cummings v. Missouri","cite":"71 U.S. 277","y":1866,"id":"1850-1900/71us277","sc":1,"sum":"Field, writing for a divided Court, held that both state and national oath laws were unconstitutional. The oath laws transformed acts that had not been forbidden into crimes and increased the punishment of acts that were known to be crimes. The oath laws were also bills of attainder since they were legislative acts that inflicted punishment without the benefit of a trial by a judge.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dep’t of Revenue of Mont. v. Kurth Ranch","cite":"511 U.S. 767","y":1994,"id":"1993/93-144","sc":1,"sum":"Montana law enforeement officers raided the farm of respondents-members of the extended Kurth family-arrested them, and confiscated and later destroyed their marijuana plants. After the Kurths pleaded guilty to drug charges, petitioner revenue department attempted, in a separate proceeding, to collect a state tax imposed on the possession and storage of dangerous drugs. That tax is collected only after any state or …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17, 43","1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dolan v. United States","cite":"560 U.S. 605","y":2010,"id":"2009/09-367","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that federal courts may enter a restitution order beyond the time prescribed under the Mandatory Victims Restitution Act if certain qualifications are met.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"33","1":"7","2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"FDIC v. Dover","cite":"453 F.3d 710","y":2006,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/453/710/","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Feltner v. Columbia Pictures Television, Inc.","cite":"523 U.S. 340","y":1998,"id":"1997/96-1768","sc":1,"ar":4,"sum":"The Court held that, despite section 504(c) of the Copyright Act's silence, the Seventh Amendment provides the right to a jury trial, which includes a right to a jury determination of the amount of statutory damages.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hudson v. United States","cite":"522 U.S. 93","y":1997,"id":"1997/96-976","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Double Jeopardy Clause does not apply to non-criminal penalties, even if the purpose of the penalty is to punish the offenders and deter future offenders. Since the first punishment handed down by the OCC - a Federal Banking Agency, not a court - was not a criminal punishment, the subsequent indictments were upheld.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"18, 37, 43-44","2":"1, 2, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hughey v. United States","cite":"495 U.S. 411","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-5691","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"A VWPA restitution award is authorized only for the loss caused by the specific conduct that is the basis of the offense of conviction.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"5, 11, 15, 27, 33, 45, 28","2":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jackson v. Birmingham Bd. of Educ.","cite":"544 U.S. 167","y":2005,"id":"2004/02-1672","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that Title IX allowed suits alleging retaliation for reporting sex discrimination. Such retaliation, the majority reasoned, constituted intentional discrimination on the basis of sex in violation of Title IX. Jackson therefore had the right under Title IX to pursue his claim in court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johannessen v. United States","cite":"225 U.S. 227","y":1912,"id":"scdb/1911-067","sc":1,"sum":"Prior decisions of this court holding that a judgment of a competent court admitting a person to citizenship is, like every other judgment, competent evidence of its own validity, go no further than protecting the judgment from collateral attack.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kansas v. Hendricks","cite":"521 U.S. 346","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-1649","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Despite Hendricks' claim that a certification of \"mental illness\" alone was too arbitrary to sustain a civil commitment order, the Court held that the Act met substantive due process standards by requiring considerable evidence of past violent sexual behavior and a present mental inclination to repeat such offenses.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"15, 17, 24, 36, 42-45","1":"4, 12, 14-17","2":"15, 20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kellogg Brown & Root Servs., Inc. v. United States ex rel. Carter","cite":"575 U.S. 650","y":2015,"id":"2014/12-1497","sc":1,"sum":"The Wartime Suspension of Limitations Act's statute of limitations tolling provision does not apply to claims of civil fraud brought by private parties. The False Claims Act's \"first-to-file bar\" keeps new claims out of court only while related claims are still alive, but not in perpetuity.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kelly v. Robinson","cite":"479 U.S. 36","y":1986,"id":"1986/85-1033","sc":1,"sum":"Section 523(a)(7) preserves from discharge in Chapter 7 any condition a state criminal court imposes as part of a criminal sentence. Thus, restitution obligations, imposed as conditions of probation in state criminal proceedings, are not dischargeable.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"3, 11, 23-24, 27, 32, 44","1":"13, 22, 23, 25, 27, 28","2":"7, 9, 12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kennedy v. Mendoza-Martinez","cite":"372 U.S. 144","y":1963,"id":"1961/2","sc":1,"op":3,"ar":2,"sum":"The government cannot divest citizenship on the sole basis of draft evasion","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"37-38, 41","1":"21","2":"15, 17, 20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kring v. Missouri","cite":"107 U.S. 221","y":1883,"id":"scdb/1882-172","sc":1,"sum":"1. A.' was convicted of murder in the first degree, and the judgment of condemnation was affirmed by the Supreme Court of Missouri. A previous sentence pronounced on his plea of guilty of murder in the second degree, and subjecting him to an imprisonment for twenty-five years, had, on his appeal, been reversed and.set aside. By the law of Missouri in force when the homicide was committed this sentence was an …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lindsey v. Washington","cite":"301 U.S. 397","y":1937,"id":"scdb/1936-123","sc":1,"sum":"A state statute making more onerous the standard of punishment is ex post facto and void as applied to a crime committed before its enactment. P.:401. Under the law of the State of Washington at the time of the alleged offense, imprisonment of the accused could have been fixed by the judge at less than the maximum of 15 years. By the law as amended and applied (c. 114, L. Wash., 1935), sentence for 15 years was made …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Liu v. SEC","cite":"591 U.S. 71","y":2020,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/591/71/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lord Townshend v. Hughes","cite":"86 Eng. Rep. 994","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Eng.%20Rep./86/994/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lynce v. Mathis","cite":"519 U.S. 433","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-7452","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that the 1992 statute canceling provisional release credits violates the Ex Post Facto Clause of the Federal Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"19, 21, 32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Manrique v. United States","cite":"581 U.S. 116","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-7250","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that appellate courts do not have jurisdiction over an appeal of a restitution award when the judgment that awarded restitution was entered after the notice of appeal was filed.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16","1":"19","2":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ogden v. Saunders","cite":"25 U.S. 213","y":1827,"id":"1789-1850/25us213","sc":1,"sum":"This is not a violation of the Contracts Clause. The state law remains controlling. The obligation of a contract made after the enactment of a bankruptcy statute is subject to the bankruptcy statute provisions. In effect, the bankruptcy statute becomes part of all subsequent contracts, limiting their obligation but not impairing them.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"One Lot Emerald Cut Stones and One Ring v. United States","cite":"409 U.S. 232","y":1972,"id":"1972/72-376","sc":1,"sum":"A forfeiture of imported merchandise not included in a declaration and entry pursuant to the tariff provision in 19 U. S. C. § 1497 is not barred by a prior acquittal under 18 U. S. C. § 545, which (unlike the civil forfeiture proceeding) requires proof of an intent to defraud; nor is the forfeiture action barred by the Double Jeopardy Clause, since Congress may impose both a criminal and civil sanction respecting …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pa. Dep’t of Pub. Welfare v. Davenport","cite":"495 U.S. 552","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-156","sc":1,"sum":"The Code's language and structure demonstrate that restitution obligations constitute \"debts\" within the meaning of § 101(11) and are therefore dischargeable under Chapter 13.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Paroline v. United States","cite":"572 U.S. 434","y":2014,"id":"2013/12-8561","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":3,"sum":"The Court ruled that to recover restitution, either the government or the victim must establish a causal relationship between the defendant's conduct and the victim's harm or damages.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"4, 8, 10, 17-18, 23, 30, 33, 30","1":"10, 11, 14, 16, 25","2":"8, 9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pasquantino v. United States","cite":"544 U.S. 349","y":2005,"id":"2004/03-725","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the law's plain language criminalized the smuggling scheme in this case. The common-law revenue rule did not bar the prosecution of the scheme, because no common-law case as of 1952 clearly established that the rule barred the U.S. from prosecuting a fraudulent scheme to evade foreign taxes.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"3, 10, 22, 29-30, 45, 30","1":"4, 10, 13, 24, 25","2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"S. Union Co. v. United States","cite":"567 U.S. 343","y":2012,"id":"2011/11-94","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the rule applied in Apprendi v. New Jersey, where the Sixth Amendment’s guarantee of a jury trial requires that any fact which increases the maximum punishment for a particular crime be proved to a jury, applies to the imposition of criminal fines.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"32","2":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Seling v. Young","cite":"531 U.S. 250","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1185","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that because the Washington Community Protection Act of 1990 had been found to be civil, it could not be deemed punitive as applied to Young for the purposes of double jeopardy and ex post facto challenges.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Smith v. Doe","cite":"538 U.S. 84","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-729","sc":1,"op":12,"sum":"Explaining that Mendoza-Martinez factors “migrated” to ex post facto jurisprudence.","sumSrc":"opinion","by":{"0":"10, 14, 17, 20, 24, 31, 36-39, 41-42, 45","1":"5, 12, 14, 16-19, 26, 27","2":"1-3, 5-6, 10-11, 13, 15, 18, 20-21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Turkiye Halk Bankasi A.S. v. United States","cite":"598 U.S. 264","y":2023,"id":"2022/21-1450","sc":1,"sum":"The District Court has jurisdiction of this criminal prosecution under 18 U. S. C. §3231, and the Foreign Sovereign Immunities Act’s comprehensive scheme governing claims of immunity in civil actions against foreign states and their instrumentalities does not cover criminal cases.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bajakajian","cite":"524 U.S. 321","y":1998,"id":"1997/96-1487","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 opinion, the Court held that the government's ability to extract fines is limited by the Excessive Fines Clause. The forfeiture of currency, when ordered for a violation of a reporting statute, is a punitive measure which constitutes a \"fine\" under the Eighth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17","1":"21","2":"3, 4, 5"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Carruth","cite":"418 F.3d 900","y":2005,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/418/900/","by":{"0":"31","1":"10, 26, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Hankins","cite":"858 F.3d 1273","y":2017,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/858/1273/","by":{"0":"33-34"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Hansen","cite":"599 U.S. 762","y":2023,"id":"2022/22-179","sc":1,"sum":"Title 8 U. S. C. §1324(a)(1)(A)(iv)—which criminalizes “encouraging or inducing” illegal immigration—forbids only the purposeful solicitation and facilitation of specific acts known to violate federal law and is not unconstitutionally overbroad.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Newman","cite":"144 F.3d 531","y":1998,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/144/531/","sum":"Stating that restitution “historically” operated as “an equitable, remedial measure designed to prevent the unjust enrichment of wrongdoers”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"38, 40-44, 46"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. One Assortment of 89 Firearms","cite":"465 U.S. 354","y":1984,"id":"1983/82-1047","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"A gun owner's acquittal on criminal charges involving firearms does not preclude a subsequent in rem forfeiture proceeding against those firearms under § 924(d).","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"16-18, 26, 37","1":"19, 20","2":"1, 6, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Rizk","cite":"660 F.3d 1125","y":2011,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/660/1125/","by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Salerno","cite":"481 U.S. 739","y":1987,"id":"1986/86-87","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Act was constitutional because when the government's interest in protecting the community outweighs individual liberty, pre-trial detention can be \"a potential solution to a pressing societal problem.\" The Act only applied to a specific list of serious offenses, placed heavy burdens on the government to prove that the arrestee posed significant threats to others, and did not prevent the accused from enjoying a speedy trial.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Ursery","cite":"518 U.S. 267","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-345","sc":1,"sum":"Chief Justice William H. Rehnquist, in an 8-to-1 decision, held that civil property forfeitures did not constitute a \"punishment\" for purposes of the double jeopardy clause. While the double jeopardy clause protects a defendant from being punished twice for the same offense, criminal and civil sanctions for the same offense are distinguishable.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"9, 18","1":"20","2":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Weaver v. Graham","cite":"450 U.S. 24","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-5780","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Held.: A Florida statute repealing an earlier statute and reducing the amount of \"gain time\" for good conduct and obedience to prison rules deducted from a convicted prisoner's sentence is unconstitutional as an ex post facto law as applied to petitioner, whose crime was committed before the statute's enactment.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13, 19, 32","1":"10"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I, Section 9, Clause 3","clauses":[["attainder-ex-post-facto","Bills of Attainder and Ex Post Facto Laws"]],"text":"No Bill of Attainder or ex post facto Law shall be passed.","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 100-690","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/102/4181","by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"law","label":"Antiterrorism and Effective Death Penalty Act (AEDPA), Pub. L. No. 104-132","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/104/public/132","by":{"0":"4, 24, 2-15, 18-25, 27-34, 36, 38-46","1":"4","2":"7, 11, 21"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 111-237","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/111/public/237","by":{"0":"31","2":"12"}},{"t":"law","label":"First Step Act, Pub. L. No. 115-391","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/115/public/391","by":{"0":"39"}},{"t":"law","label":"Sentencing Reform Act, Pub. L. No. 98-473","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/98/1837","by":{"0":"29"}},{"t":"law","label":"and Rules—continued: Victim and Witness Protection Act (VWPA), Pub. L. No. 97-291","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/96/1248","by":{"0":"4-5, 11, 15, 27-28, 33-34, 45","1":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"11 U.S.C. § 523","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/11/523","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 25","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/25","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3147","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3147","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 856","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/856","by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"usc","label":"26 U.S.C. § 6201","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/6201","by":{"0":"12"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 608","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/608","by":{"0":"39"}},{"t":"reg","label":"28 C.F.R. § 545.11","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-28/section-545.11","by":{"0":"39"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Crim. P. 32","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcrmp/rule_32","subs":["(c)"],"by":{"0":"6, 18-19","1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"U.S. Brief, Edwards v. United States","cite":"162 F.3d 87","y":1998,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/162/87/","by":{"0":"12, 30","1":"21, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"U.S. Brief, Robers v. United States","cite":"572 U.S. 639","id":"2013/12-9012","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that, under the Mandatory Victims Restitution Act, individuals accused of property crime must pay “an amount equal to...the value of the property,” minus “the value...of any part of the property that is returned\" in retribution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"U.S. Brief in Opposition, Dantone, Inc. v. United States","cite":"549 U.S. 1071","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/549/1071/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Other Authorities—continued: U.S. Brief in Opposition, Hankins v. United States","cite":"583 U.S. 1054","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/583/1054/","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":1,"by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Holguin-Hernandez v. United States","cite":"589 U.S. 169","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-7739","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a criminal defendant's district-court argument for a specific sentence preserved his claim on appeal that the sentence imposed was unreasonably long.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Peugh v. United States","cite":"569 U.S. 530","y":2013,"id":"2012/12-62","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court found that using the U.S. Sentencing Guidelines at the time of the sentencing for a crime rather than at the time of the offense is a violation of the ex post facto clause.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rutledge v. United States","cite":"517 U.S. 292","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-8769","sc":1,"sum":"Announced by Justice John Paul Stevens, the Court ruled one of the two sentences must be dropped. Both of the offenses were based on the same criminal act. Justice Stevens wrote, \"A guilty verdict on a (continuing criminal enterprise) charge necessarily includes a finding that the defendant also participated in a conspiracy.\".","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stogner v. California","cite":"539 U.S. 607","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-1757","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that a law enacted after expiration of a previously applicable limitations period violates the Ex Post Facto Clause when it is applied to revive a previously time-barred prosecution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taggart v. Lorenzen","cite":"587 U.S. 554","y":2019,"id":"2018/18-489","sc":1,"sum":"A creditor may be held in civil contempt for violating a bankruptcy court’s discharge order if there is no fair ground of doubt as to whether the order barred the creditor’s conduct.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"TC Heartland LLC v. Kraft Foods Grp. Brands LLC","cite":"581 U.S. 258","y":2017,"id":"2016/16-341","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the subsection of the general venue statute that allows a corporation to reside in many jurisdictions for the purpose of establishing jurisdiction does not apply to the patent venue statute.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Anthony","cite":"25 F.4th 792","y":2022,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.4th/25/792/","sum":"Noting that Paroline had “call[ed] into question” circuit precedent holding that “the MVRA lacks a penal element”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bapack","cite":"129 F.3d 1320","y":1997,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/129/1320/","sum":"Observing that “retroactive application of mandatory restitution provisions raises ex post facto concerns”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Baggett","cite":"125 F.3d 1319","y":1997,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/125/1319/","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Blackwell","cite":"852 F.3d 1164","y":2017,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/852/1164/","by":{"1":"30, 31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Brown","cite":"744 F.2d 905","y":1984,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/744/905/","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Florence","cite":"741 F.2d 1066","y":1984,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/741/1066/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Keith","cite":"754 F.2d 1388","y":1984,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/754/1388/","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. McGuire","cite":"636 F. Appx. 445","y":2016,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Appx./636/445/","by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Norwood","cite":"49 F.4th 189","y":2022,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.4th/49/189/","by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Palma","cite":"760 F.2d 475","y":1985,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/760/475/","by":{"1":"13, 22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Richards","cite":"204 F.3d 177","y":2000,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/204/177/","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Rosello","cite":"737 Fed. Appx. 907","y":2018,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Fed.%20Appx./737/907/","by":{"1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Satterfield","cite":"743 F.2d 827","y":1984,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/743/827/","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Schulte","cite":"264 F.3d 656","y":2001,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/264/656/","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Siegel","cite":"153 F.3d 1256","y":1998,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/153/1256/","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Thunderhawk","cite":"799 F.3d 1203","y":2015,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/799/1203/","by":{"1":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Watchman","cite":"749 F.2d 616","y":1984,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/749/616/","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Ward","cite":"448 U.S. 242","y":1980,"id":"1979/79-394","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Section 311 (b) (3) of the Federal Water Pollution Control Act prohibits the discharge of oil into navigable waters. Section 311 (b) (5) requires any person m charge of an onshore facility to report any such discharge to the appropriate Government agency, and a failure to report subjects the person to a fine or imprisonment. Section 311 (b) (5) also provides for a form of \"use immunity,\" by specifying that …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"16","2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Weinlein","cite":"109 F.4th 91","y":2024,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.4th/109/91/","by":{"1":"30, 31"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Witham","cite":"648 F.3d 40","y":2011,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/648/40/","by":{"1":"7, 20"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 114-324","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/114/public/324","by":{"1":"8"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 2248","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2248","by":{"1":"8"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 924","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/924","subs":["(c)(1994)","(d)"],"stext":{"head":"§924. Penalties","paras":[[0,"(a)(1) Except as otherwise provided in this subsection, subsection (b), (c), (f), or (p) of this section, or in section 929, whoever—"],[1,"(A) knowingly makes any false statement or representation with respect to the information required by this chapter to be kept in the records of a person licensed under this chapter or in applying for any license or exemption or relief from disability under the provisions of this chapter;"],[1,"(B) knowingly violates subsection (a)(4), (f), (k), or (q) of section 922;"],[1,"(C) knowingly imports or brings into the United States or any possession thereof any firearm or ammunition in violation of section 922(l); or"],[1,"(D) willfully violates any other provision of this chapter,"],[0,"shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than five years, or both."],[0,"(2) Whoever knowingly violates subsection (a)(6), (h), (i), (j), or (o) of section 922 shall be fined as provided in this title, imprisoned not more than 10 years, or both."],[0,"(3) Any licensed dealer, licensed importer, licensed manufacturer, or licensed collector who knowingly—"],[1,"(A) makes any false statement or representation with respect to the information required by the provisions of this chapter to be kept in the records of a person licensed under this chapter, or"],[1,"(B) violates subsection (m) of section 922,"],[0,"shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than one year, or both."],[0,"(4) Whoever violates section 922(q) shall be fined under this title, imprisoned for not more than 5 years, or both. Notwithstanding any other provision of law, the term of imprisonment imposed under this paragraph shall not run concurrently with any other term of imprisonment imposed under any other provision of law. Except for the authorization of a term of imprisonment of not more than 5 years made in this paragraph, for the purpose of any other law a violation of section 922(q) shall be deemed to be a misdemeanor."],[0,"(5) Whoever knowingly violates subsection (s) or (t) of section 922 shall be fined under this title, imprisoned for not more than 1 year, or both."],[0,"(6)(A)(i) A juvenile who violates section 922(x) shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 1 year, or both, except that a juvenile described in clause (ii) shall be sentenced to probation on appropriate conditions and shall not be incarcerated unless the juvenile fails to comply with a condition of probation."],[0,"(ii) A juvenile is described in this clause if—"],[1,"(I) the offense of which the juvenile is charged is possession of a handgun or ammunition in violation of section 922(x)(2); and"],[1,"(II) the juvenile has not been convicted in any court of an offense (including an offense under section 922(x) or a similar State law, but not including any other offense consisting of conduct that if engaged in by an adult would not constitute an offense) or adjudicated as a juvenile delinquent for conduct that if engaged in by an adult would constitute an offense."],[0,"(B) A person other than a juvenile who knowingly violates section 922(x)—"],[1,"(i) shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 1 year, or both; and"],[1,"(ii) if the person sold, delivered, or otherwise transferred a handgun or ammunition to a juvenile knowing or having reasonable cause to know that the juvenile intended to carry or otherwise possess or discharge or otherwise use the handgun or ammunition in the commission of a crime of violence, shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 10 years, or both."],[0,"(7) Whoever knowingly violates section 931 shall be fined under this title, imprisoned not more than 3 years, or both."],[0,"(8) Whoever knowingly violates subsection (d) or (g) of section 922 shall be fined under this title, imprisoned for not more than 15 years, or both."],[0,"(b) Whoever, with intent to commit therewith an offense punishable by imprisonment for a term exceeding one year, or with knowledge or reasonable cause to believe that an offense punishable by imprisonment for a term exceeding one year is to be committed therewith, ships, transports, or receives a firearm or any ammunition in interstate or foreign commerce shall be fined under this title, or imprisoned not more than ten years, or both."],[0,"(c)(1)(A) Except to the extent that a greater minimum sentence is otherwise provided by this subsection or by any other provision of law, any person who, during and in relation to any crime of violence or drug trafficking crime (including a crime of violence or drug trafficking crime that provides for an enhanced punishment if committed by the use of a deadly or dangerous weapon or device) for which the person may be prosecuted in a court of the United States, uses or carries a firearm, or who, in furtherance of any such crime, possesses a firearm, shall, in addition to the punishment provided for such crime of violence or drug trafficking crime—"],[1,"(i) be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 5 years;"],[1,"(ii) if the firearm is brandished, be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 7 years; and"],[1,"(iii) if the firearm is discharged, be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 10 years."],[0,"(B) If the firearm possessed by a person convicted of a violation of this subsection—"],[1,"(i) is a short-barreled rifle, short-barreled shotgun, or semiautomatic assault weapon, the person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 10 years; or"],[1,"(ii) is a machinegun or a destructive device, or is equipped with a firearm silencer or firearm muffler, the person shall be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 30 years."],[0,"(C) In the case of a violation of this subsection that occurs after a prior conviction under this subsection has become final, the person shall—"],[1,"(i) be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 25 years; and"],[1,"(ii) if the firearm involved is a machinegun or a destructive device, or is equipped with a firearm silencer or firearm muffler, be sentenced to imprisonment for life."],[0,"(D) Notwithstanding any other provision of law—"],[1,"(i) a court shall not place on probation any person convicted of a violation of this subsection; and"],[1,"(ii) no term of imprisonment imposed on a person under this subsection shall run concurrently with any other term of imprisonment imposed on the person, including any term of imprisonment imposed for the crime of violence or drug trafficking crime during which the firearm was used, carried, or possessed."],[0,"(2) For purposes of this subsection, the term \"drug trafficking crime\" means any felony punishable under the Controlled Substances Act (21 U.S.C. 801 et seq.), the Controlled Substances Import and Export Act (21 U.S.C. 951 et seq.), or chapter 705 of title 46."],[0,"(3) For purposes of this subsection the term \"crime of violence\" means an offense that is a felony and—"],[1,"(A) has as an element the use, attempted use, or threatened use of physical force against the person or property of another, or"],[1,"(B) that by its nature, involves a substantial risk that physical force against the person or property of another may be used in the course of committing the offense."],[0,"(4) For purposes of this subsection, the term \"brandish\" means, with respect to a firearm, to display all or part of the firearm, or otherwise make the presence of the firearm known to another person, in order to intimidate that person, regardless of whether the firearm is directly visible to that person."],[0,"(5) Except to the extent that a greater minimum sentence is otherwise provided under this subsection, or by any other provision of law, any person who, during and in relation to any crime of violence or drug trafficking crime (including a crime of violence or drug trafficking crime that provides for an enhanced punishment if committed by the use of a deadly or dangerous weapon or device) for which the person may be prosecuted in a court of the United States, uses or carries armor piercing ammunition, or who, in furtherance of any such crime, possesses armor piercing ammunition, shall, in addition to the punishment provided for such crime of violence or drug trafficking crime or conviction under this section—"],[1,"(A) be sentenced to a term of imprisonment of not less than 15 years; and"],[1,"(B) if death results from the use of such ammunition—"],[2,"(i) if the killing is murder (as defined in section 1111), be punished by death or sentenced to a term of imprisonment for any term of years or for life; and"],[2,"(ii) if the killing is manslaughter (as defined in section 1112), be punished as provided in section 1112."],[0,"(d)(1) Any firearm or ammunition involved in or used in any knowing violation of subsection (a)(4), (a)(6), (f), (g), (h), (i), (j), or (k) of section 922, or knowing importation or bringing into the United States or any possession thereof any firearm or ammunition in violation of section 922(l), or knowing violation of section 924, 932, or 933, or willful violation of any other provision of this chapter or any rule or regulation promulgated thereunder, or any violation of any other criminal law of the United States, or any firearm or ammunition intended to be used in any offense referred to in paragraph (3) of this subsection, where such intent is demonstrated by clear and convincing evidence, shall be subject to seizure and forfeiture, and all provisions of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 relating to the seizure, forfeiture, and disposition of firearms, as defined in section 5845(a) of that Code, shall, so far as applicable, extend to seizures and forfeitures under the provisions of this chapter: Provided, That upon acquittal of the owner or possessor, or dismissal of the charges against him other than upon motion of the Government prior to trial, or lapse of or court termination of the restraining order to which he is subject, the seized or relinquished firearms or ammunition shall be returned forthwith to the owner or possessor or to a person delegated by the owner or possessor unless the return of the firearms or ammunition would place the owner or possessor or his delegate in violation of law. Any action or proceeding for the forfeiture of firearms or ammunition shall be commenced within one hundred and twenty days of such seizure."]],"more":true,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/924?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§924. Penalties","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"1":"5, 9, 20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1365","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1365","by":{"1":"7"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 2113","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2113","subs":["(a)(1994)","(d)(1994)"],"by":{"1":"5, 9, 5, 9"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 2259","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2259","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3551","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3551","subs":["(b)","(c)"],"by":{"1":"28, 28, 28"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3553","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3553","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3556","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3556","stext":{"head":"§3556. Order of restitution","paras":[[0,"The court, in imposing a sentence on a defendant who has been found guilty of an offense shall order restitution in accordance with section 3663A, and may order restitution in accordance with section 3663. The procedures under section 3664 shall apply to all orders of restitution under this section."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/3556?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"ar":1,"head":"§3556. Order of restitution","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3563","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3563","subs":["(a)","(a)(6)","(a)(6)(A)"],"by":{"1":"18, 8, 12, 4"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3572","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3572","subs":["(d)"],"by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3583","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3583","subs":["(d)(1996)","(b)(2)(1996)","(d)"],"by":{"1":"8, 8, 7"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3611","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3611","by":{"1":"7, 18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3612","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3612","subs":["(c)","(d)","(e)"],"by":{"1":"20, 20, 20, 20, 8, 8"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3613","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3613","subs":["(b)","(b)(1)(1994)"],"by":{"1":"8, 30, 6, 8, 13, 20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3613A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3613A","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"1":"8, 13, 20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3614","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3614","subs":["(a)","(b)"],"stext":{"head":"§3614. Resentencing upon failure to pay a fine or restitution","paras":[[0,"(a) Resentencing.—Subject to the provisions of subsection (b), if a defendant knowingly fails to pay a delinquent fine or restitution the court may resentence the defendant to any sentence which might originally have been imposed."],[0,"(b) Imprisonment.—The defendant may be sentenced to a term of imprisonment under subsection (a) only if the court determines that—"],[1,"(1) the defendant willfully refused to pay the delinquent fine or had failed to make sufficient bona fide efforts to pay the fine; or"],[1,"(2) in light of the nature of the offense and the characteristics of the person, alternatives to imprisonment are not adequate to serve the purposes of punishment and deterrence."],[0,"(c) Effect of Indigency.—In no event shall a defendant be incarcerated under this section solely on the basis of inability to make payments because the defendant is indigent."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/3614?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"ar":1,"head":"§3614. Resentencing upon failure to pay a fine or restitution","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"1":"20, 20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3663","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3663","subs":["(a)(1)(1994)","(a)(1)(A)","(a)(1)(B)","(a)(3)","(c)(1)","(h)(1)(1994)"],"stext":{"head":"§3663. Order of restitution","paras":[[0,"(a)(1)(A) The court, when sentencing a defendant convicted of an offense under this title, section 401, 408(a), 409, 416, 420, or 422(a) of the Controlled Substances Act (21 U.S.C. 841, 848(a), 849, 856, 861, 863) (but in no case shall a participant in an offense under such sections be considered a victim of such offense under this section), or section 5124, 46312, 46502, or 46504 of title 49, other than an offense described in section 3663A(c), may order, in addition to or, in the case of a misdemeanor, in lieu of any other penalty authorized by law, that the defendant make restitution to any victim of such offense, or if the victim is deceased, to the victim's estate. The court may also order, if agreed to by the parties in a plea agreement, restitution to persons other than the victim of the offense."],[0,"(B)(i) The court, in determining whether to order restitution under this section, shall consider—"],[1,"(I) the amount of the loss sustained by each victim as a result of the offense; and"],[1,"(II) the financial resources of the defendant, the financial needs and earning ability of the defendant and the defendant's dependents, and such other factors as the court deems appropriate."],[0,"(ii) To the extent that the court determines that the complication and prolongation of the sentencing process resulting from the fashioning of an order of restitution under this section outweighs the need to provide restitution to any victims, the court may decline to make such an order."],[0,"(2) For the purposes of this section, the term \"victim\" means a person directly and proximately harmed as a result of the commission of an offense for which restitution may be ordered including, in the case of an offense that involves as an element a scheme, conspiracy, or pattern of criminal activity, any person directly harmed by the defendant's criminal conduct in the course of the scheme, conspiracy, or pattern. In the case of a victim who is under 18 years of age, incompetent, incapacitated, or deceased, the legal guardian of the victim or representative of the victim's estate, another family member, or any other person appointed as suitable by the court, may assume the victim's rights under this section, but in no event shall the defendant be named as such representative or guardian."],[0,"(3) The court may also order restitution in any criminal case to the extent agreed to by the parties in a plea agreement."],[0,"(b) The order may require that such defendant—"],[1,"(1) in the case of an offense resulting in damage to or loss or destruction of property of a victim of the offense—"],[2,"(A) return the property to the owner of the property or someone designated by the owner; or"],[2,"(B) if return of the property under subparagraph (A) is impossible, impractical, or inadequate, pay an amount equal to the greater of—"],[3,"(i) the value of the property on the date of the damage, loss, or destruction, or"],[3,"(ii) the value of the property on the date of sentencing,"],[1,"less the value (as of the date the property is returned) of any part of the property that is returned;"],[1,"(2) in the case of an offense resulting in bodily injury to a victim including an offense under chapter 109A or chapter 110—"],[2,"(A) pay an amount equal to the cost of necessary medical and related professional services and devices relating to physical, psychiatric, and psychological care, including nonmedical care and treatment rendered in accordance with a method of healing recognized by the law of the place of treatment;"],[2,"(B) pay an amount equal to the cost of necessary physical and occupational therapy and rehabilitation; and"],[2,"(C) reimburse the victim for income lost by such victim as a result of such offense;"],[1,"(3) in the case of an offense resulting in bodily injury also results in the death of a victim, pay an amount equal to the cost of necessary funeral and related services;"],[1,"(4) in any case, reimburse the victim for lost income and necessary child care, transportation, and other expenses related to participation in the investigation or prosecution of the offense or attendance at proceedings related to the offense;"],[1,"(5) in any case, if the victim (or if the victim is deceased, the victim's estate) consents, make restitution in services in lieu of money, or make restitution to a person or organization designated by the victim or the estate; and"],[1,"(6) in the case of an offense under sections 1028(a)(7) or 1028A(a) of this title, pay an amount equal to the value of the time reasonably spent by the victim in an attempt to remediate the intended or actual harm incurred by the victim from the offense."],[0,"(c)(1) Notwithstanding any other provision of law (but subject to the provisions of subsections (a)(1)(B)(i)(II) and (ii),1 when sentencing a defendant convicted of an offense described in section 401, 408(a), 409, 416, 420, or 422(a) of the Controlled Substances Act (21 U.S.C. 841, 848(a), 849, 856, 861, 863), in which there is no identifiable victim, the court may order that the defendant make restitution in accordance with this subsection."],[0,"(2)(A) An order of restitution under this subsection shall be based on the amount of public harm caused by the offense, as determined by the court in accordance with guidelines promulgated by the United States Sentencing Commission."],[0,"(B) In no case shall the amount of restitution ordered under this subsection exceed the amount of the fine which may be ordered for the offense charged in the case."],[0,"(3) Restitution under this subsection shall be distributed as follows:"],[1,"(A) 65 percent of the total amount of restitution shall be paid to the State entity designated to administer crime victim assistance in the State in which the crime occurred."],[1,"(B) 35 percent of the total amount of restitution shall be paid to the State entity designated to receive Federal substance abuse block grant funds."],[0,"(4) The court shall not make an award under this subsection if it appears likely that such award would interfere with a forfeiture under chapter 46 or chapter 96 of this title or under the Controlled Substances Act (21 U.S.C. 801 et seq.)."],[0,"(5) Notwithstanding section 3612(c) or any other provision of law, a penalty assessment under section 3013 or a fine under subchapter C of chapter 227 shall take precedence over an order of restitution under this subsection."],[0,"(6) Requests for community restitution under this subsection may be considered in all plea agreements negotiated by the United States."],[0,"(7)(A) The United States Sentencing Commission shall promulgate guidelines to assist courts in determining the amount of restitution that may be ordered under this subsection."],[0,"(B) No restitution shall be ordered under this subsection until such time as the Sentencing Commission promulgates guidelines pursuant to this paragraph."],[0,"(d) An order of restitution made pursuant to this section shall be issued and enforced in accordance with section 3664."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/3663?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"ar":3,"head":"§3663. Order of restitution","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"1":"6, 22, 6, 22, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3663A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3663A","subs":["(c)(1)(B)","(a)(1)","(a)(3)","(c)(3)(B)","(d)"],"stext":{"head":"§3663A. Mandatory restitution to victims of certain crimes","paras":[[0,"(a)(1) Notwithstanding any other provision of law, when sentencing a defendant convicted of an offense described in subsection (c), the court shall order, in addition to, or in the case of a misdemeanor, in addition to or in lieu of, any other penalty authorized by law, that the defendant make restitution to the victim of the offense or, if the victim is deceased, to the victim's estate."],[0,"(2) For the purposes of this section, the term \"victim\" means a person directly and proximately harmed as a result of the commission of an offense for which restitution may be ordered including, in the case of an offense that involves as an element a scheme, conspiracy, or pattern of criminal activity, any person directly harmed by the defendant's criminal conduct in the course of the scheme, conspiracy, or pattern. In the case of a victim who is under 18 years of age, incompetent, incapacitated, or deceased, the legal guardian of the victim or representative of the victim's estate, another family member, or any other person appointed as suitable by the court, may assume the victim's rights under this section, but in no event shall the defendant be named as such representative or guardian."],[0,"(3) The court shall also order, if agreed to by the parties in a plea agreement, restitution to persons other than the victim of the offense."],[0,"(4) Clarification.—In ordering restitution under this section, a court shall order the defendant to make restitution to a person who has assumed the victim's rights under paragraph (2) to reimburse that person's necessary and reasonable—"],[1,"(A) lost income, child care, transportation, and other expenses incurred during and directly related to participation in the investigation or prosecution of the offense or attendance at proceedings related to the offense;"],[1,"(B) lost income, transportation, and other expenses incurred that are directly related to transporting the victim for necessary medical and related professional services and devices relating to physical, psychiatric, and psychological care, including nonmedical care and treatment rendered in accordance with a method of healing recognized by the law of the place of treatment; and"],[1,"(C) lost income, transportation, and other expenses incurred that are directly related to transporting the victim to receive necessary physical and occupational therapy and rehabilitation."],[0,"(b) The order of restitution shall require that such defendant—"],[1,"(1) in the case of an offense resulting in damage to or loss or destruction of property of a victim of the offense—"],[2,"(A) return the property to the owner of the property or someone designated by the owner; or"],[2,"(B) if return of the property under subparagraph (A) is impossible, impracticable, or inadequate, pay an amount equal to—"],[3,"(i) the greater of—"],[4,"(I) the value of the property on the date of the damage, loss, or destruction; or"],[4,"(II) the value of the property on the date of sentencing, less"],[3,"(ii) the value (as of the date the property is returned) of any part of the property that is returned;"],[1,"(2) in the case of an offense resulting in bodily injury to a victim—"],[2,"(A) pay an amount equal to the cost of necessary medical and related professional services and devices relating to physical, psychiatric, and psychological care, including nonmedical care and treatment rendered in accordance with a method of healing recognized by the law of the place of treatment;"],[2,"(B) pay an amount equal to the cost of necessary physical and occupational therapy and rehabilitation; and"],[2,"(C) reimburse the victim for income lost by such victim as a result of such offense;"],[1,"(3) in the case of an offense resulting in bodily injury that results in the death of the victim, pay an amount equal to the cost of necessary funeral and related services; and"],[1,"(4) in any case, reimburse the victim for lost income and necessary child care, transportation, and other expenses incurred during participation in the investigation or prosecution of the offense or attendance at proceedings related to the offense."],[0,"(c)(1) This section shall apply in all sentencing proceedings for convictions of, or plea agreements relating to charges for, any offense—"],[1,"(A) that is—"],[2,"(i) a crime of violence, as defined in section 16;"],[2,"(ii) an offense against property under this title, or under section 416(a) of the Controlled Substances Act (21 U.S.C. 856(a)), including any offense committed by fraud or deceit;"],[2,"(iii) an offense described in section 3 of the Rodchenkov Anti-Doping Act of 2019;"],[2,"(iv) an offense described in section 1365 (relating to tampering with consumer products); or"],[2,"(v) an offense under section 670 (relating to theft of medical products); and"],[1,"(B) in which an identifiable victim or victims has suffered a physical injury or pecuniary loss."],[0,"(2) In the case of a plea agreement that does not result in a conviction for an offense described in paragraph (1), this section shall apply only if the plea specifically states that an offense listed under such paragraph gave rise to the plea agreement."],[0,"(3) This section shall not apply in the case of an offense described in paragraph (1)(A)(ii) or (iii) if the court finds, from facts on the record, that—"],[1,"(A) the number of identifiable victims is so large as to make restitution impracticable; or"],[1,"(B) determining complex issues of fact related to the cause or amount of the victim's losses would complicate or prolong the sentencing process to a degree that the need to provide restitution to any victim is outweighed by the burden on the sentencing process."],[0,"(d) An order of restitution under this section shall be issued and enforced in accordance with section 3664."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/18/3663A?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"ar":1,"head":"§3663A. Mandatory restitution to victims of certain crimes","tname":"Crimes and Criminal Procedure","by":{"1":"6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6, 6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3664","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3664","subs":["(1994)","(a)","(b)","(c)","(e)","(m)(1)(A)","(m)(1)(A)(i)","(m)(1)(A)(ii)","(m)(1)(B)","(o)(1)"],"by":{"1":"6, 3, 28, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 19, 19"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3742","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3742","by":{"1":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Austin v. United States","cite":"509 US. 602","y":1993,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/US./509/602/","by":{"2":"2, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Flemming v. Nestor","cite":"363 U.S. 603","y":1960,"id":"1959/54","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Social Security system was meant to be a form of long-term social insurance that has to constantly adapt to the demands of a changing workforce, which prevents it from fitting within the framework of the right to accrued property.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Helvering v. Mitchell","cite":"303 U.S. 391","y":1938,"id":"scdb/1937-140","sc":1,"ar":3,"sum":"That an acquittal of a charge of wilful attempt to evade, under § 146 (b), does not bar assessment and collection of the 50% addition prescribed by § 293 (b)..397 et seq. The doctrine of res judicata is inapplicable because of the difference in quantum of proof in civil and criminal cases; the acquittal was merely an adjudication that the proof was not sufficient to overcome all reasonable doubt of guilt. P. 397. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"18, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jennings v. Rodriguez","cite":"583 U.S. 281","y":2018,"id":"2017/15-1204","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that non-citizens who are subject to mandatory detention under the Immigration and Nationality Act are not entitled to periodic bond hearings. with the possibility of release if they are detained for longer than six months.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Miller v. Alabama","cite":"567 U.S. 460","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-9646","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court found it to be a violation of the Eighth Amendment to impose a sentence of life imprisonment without the possibility of parole on juvenile offenders.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"9-10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Murphy v. United States","cite":"272 U.S. 630","y":1926,"id":"scdb/1926-025","sc":1,"sum":"It is declared by the National Prohibition Act, Tit. II, § 21, that any room, etc., where intoxicating liquor is manufactured, sold, or kept in violation of the statute, is a common nuisance, and maintaining it is made a misdemeanor punishable by fine or imprisonment, or both. Section 22 authorizes suits in equity by the United States, in which such nuisances shall be abated, and in which the Court may enjoin …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"5, 16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States ex rel. Marcus v. Hess","cite":"317 U.S. 537","y":1943,"id":"scdb/1942-043","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":3,"sum":"1. Section 5438 of the Revised Statutes, which subjects to the penalties therein prescribed \"Every person who.. causes to be presented, for payment... any claim upon or against the Government of the United States,... knowing such claim to be.. fraudulent...,\" held applicable to contractors who, by collusive bidding, obtained contracts with municipalities and school districts of a State for work on federal Public …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Halper","cite":"490 U.S. 435","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-1383","sc":1,"sum":"Justice Harry Blackmun, on behalf of a unanimous Supreme Court, wrote that while previous cases had held penalties under the Act to be civil in nature, that did not foreclose the possibility of the penalty being so extreme and so unrelated to the actual damages as to constitute \"punishment.\" Because Halper had already been jailed and fined, additional punishment in a separate proceeding would violate the Double Jeopardy Clause of the Fifth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"2, 13-14"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Litwok","cite":"678 F.3d 208","y":2012,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/678/208/","by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment VII","clauses":[["seventh-amendment","Seventh Amendment (Civil Jury)"]],"text":"In Suits at common law, where the value in controversy shall exceed twenty dollars, the right of trial by jury shall be preserved, and no fact tried by a jury, shall be otherwise re-examined in any Court of the United States, than according to the rules of the common law.","by":{"2":"16, 17"}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 229A","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/229A","by":{"2":"11"}},{"t":"usc","label":"34 U.S.C. § 20101","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/34/20101","by":{"2":"7"}}]};